---
import BlogArticle from "../../components/BlogArticle.astro";
---

<BlogArticle
	title="How to Reduce a PAN Card Photo to Under 50 KB Online"
	description="Step-by-step guide to cropping and compressing a PAN card photo under 50 KB while keeping it clear, and checking the current portal limit."
	canonicalUrl="https://nsdlphotoresize.com/blogs/reduce-pan-photo-under-50kb/"
	heading="How to reduce a photo to under 50 KB for a PAN card online"
	intro="A smaller file is useful only if the picture still meets the application’s image rules and remains clear. Check the current portal limit first: 50 KB is a commonly quoted legacy maximum, not a universal PAN photo requirement."
>
	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">1. Confirm the maximum file size</h2>
		<p>
			Open the live application form and note its allowed format and
			maximum size. Older NSDL/Protean guidance is often summarized as
			50 KB for a photo, while older UTIITSL instructions commonly
			quote 30 KB. Requirements may differ in the current Form 93/94
			flow, so do not select a 50 KB target just because it appears in
			an older guide.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">2. Crop before compressing</h2>
		<p>
			Remove empty space outside the required composition, straighten
			the photo, and keep the subject centered as instructed. Cropping
			can reduce unnecessary image detail while keeping the face large
			enough. Avoid aggressive cropping that cuts off the head or
			shoulders, and do not resize to arbitrary dimensions before
			confirming the current aspect ratio.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">3. Export and inspect a JPEG</h2>
		<ol class="list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6">
			<li>Open the <a class="text-link" href="/#photo-tool">PAN card photo resizer</a> and select your original image.</li>
			<li>Set the dimensions requested by the live application and adjust the crop without stretching the image.</li>
			<li>Export as JPEG if the application accepts JPEG. If the file is over the limit, reduce compression quality a small step and export again.</li>
			<li>Check the downloaded file’s actual size in your phone’s or computer’s file information. Do not rely on the image preview or rename the extension.</li>
			<li>Open the compressed file at full size. If edges look blocky, skin tones smear, or fine details disappear, restore quality and use a clearer source or more efficient crop.</li>
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section class="space-y-3">
		<h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink">Why a file may still exceed 50 KB</h2>
		<p>
			A photo with noise, a busy background, lots of fine detail, or
			unnecessarily large pixel dimensions can need more data. Choose
			a sharp source with even lighting and a plain background. Resize
			to the required output dimensions before adjusting JPEG quality.
			Do not turn the image into a tiny thumbnail merely to reach the
			KB target.
		</p>
		<p>
			If your file is still too large at acceptable quality, try a
			cleaner source photo or check whether the application allows
			another format. A file that is exactly at a stated upper limit
			may be rejected if the portal uses a strict “less than” check, so
			leave a small margin when possible—but follow the displayed rule.
		</p>
	</section>

	<p>
		This <strong>PAN card document resizer online</strong> processes your
		image in the browser and does not upload it to this site. It cannot
		guarantee the application accepts the result; verify the current
		application instructions before submission.
	</p>
</BlogArticle>
